\(2V(\chi^{0b}_{\rm AB,AB}({\bf q}_{1})+\chi^{0b}_{\rm AB,BA}({\bf q}_{1}))\) is close to unity. In fact, for \({\bf q}\approx{\bf q}_{1}\), the relation between the BO susceptibility and its irreducible susceptibility is \(\hat{\chi}^{b}=\hat{\chi}^{b0}+2V\hat{\chi}^{b0}\hat{\chi}^{b}\), where \(\hat{\chi}^{b0}\) is the \(2\times 2\) matrix: \(\hat{\chi}^{b0}=\left(\begin{matrix}\chi^{b0}_{\rm AB,AB}&\chi^{b0}_{\rm AB, BA}\\ \chi^{b0}_{\rm BA,AB}&\chi^{b0}_{\rm BA,BA}\end{matrix}\right).\) Then, the BO susceptibility for \({\bf q}\approx{\bf q}_{1}\) is obtained as

\[\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,AB}(q) = [a-2V(a^{2}-b^{2})]/d, \tag{14}\] \[\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,BA}(q) = b/d, \tag{15}\]

where \(a\equiv\chi^{0b}_{\rm AB,AB}(q)\), \(b\equiv\chi^{0b}_{\rm AB,BA}(q)\), and \(d\equiv(1-(a+b)2V)(1-(a-b)2V)\).

According to Supplementary Eqs. (14) and (15), \(R\) becomes \(b/a(\ll 1)\) when \(V=0\). In contrast, we obtain \(R\approx 1\) at \(\alpha_{\rm BO}\approx 1\) (\(\alpha_{\rm BO}=(a+b)2V\)). Supplementary Figure 3 shows the BO susceptibilities \(\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,AB}\) and \(\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,BA}\) as functions of \(V\). We set \(a=\chi^{b0}_{\rm AB,AB}({\bf q}_{1})=0.35\) and \(b/a=0.2\). Both \(\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,AB}\) and \(\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,BA}\) increase with \(V\), while \(\Delta\chi^{b}\equiv\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,AB}-\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,BA}\) is almost constant. This result means that the relation \(R\approx 1\) holds near the BO-endpoint, around which the relation \(\alpha_{\rm BO}\lesssim 1\) holds.

According to Supplementary Eqs. (11) and (13), in the case of \(R\approx 1\), the MT kernel function for the DW equation is simply given as

\[I_{ll^{\prime},mm^{\prime}}(k,k^{\prime},q) \approx-g^{m^{\prime}l^{\prime}}_{k^{\prime}-k}(k)g^{lm}_{k-k^{ \prime}}(k^{\prime}+q) \tag{16}\] \[\times 2(2V)^{2}\chi^{b}(k-k^{\prime}),\]

where \(\chi^{b}(q)\equiv\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,AB}(q)+\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,BA}(q)\approx\chi^{ b0}_{\rm AB,AB}(q)/(1-\alpha_{\rm BO}(q))\) for \({\bf q}\approx{\bf q}_{1}\), and \(g^{lm}\approx b_{lm}\) is the normalized BO form factor derived from the DW equation [1]. Supplementary Equation (16) is equal to Eq. (10) in the main text with \(y=2\). Note that both charge- and three spin-channel BO fluctuations develop in the present off-site \(V\) mechanism.

To summarize, the relation \(\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,AB}\approx\chi^{b}_{\rm AB,BA}\) (_i.e._, \(R\approx 1\)), which is assumed in the cLC mechanism in the main text, is well satisfied in the off-site \(V\) mechanism. Because this relation is also satisfied in the paramagnon-interference mechanism [1] and the phonon mechanism, these three different BO mechanisms will cooperate. We consider that the main mechanism of the BO in kagomemetals is the paramagnon interference mechanism [1], and both the bond-stretching phonon mode and the off-site Coulomb interaction will assist the BO formation.

#### Supplementary Note 2-3: The relation \(y\gtrsim 1/2\) when \(v\) and \(V\) coexist

We consider the MT kernel function in the presence of both the off-site Coulomb interaction \(V\) and the BO interaction \(v\) in Eq. (2) in the main text. In this case, \(y\gtrsim 1/2\) is expected to be realized. Considering the \(SU(2)\) symmetry in spin space, the spin indices of the MT term in Supplementary Eq. (16) are written as [45]

\[I^{\sigma\sigma^{\prime},\rho\rho^{\prime}}_{ll^{\prime},mm^{ \prime}}(k,k^{\prime},q) = \frac{1}{2}I^{\varsigma}_{ll^{\prime},mm^{\prime}}(k,k^{\prime}, q)\delta_{\sigma,\sigma^{\prime}}\delta_{\rho^{\prime},\rho} \tag{17}\] \[+\frac{1}{2}I^{\varsigma}_{ll^{\prime},mm^{\prime}}(k,k^{\prime},q)\mathbf{\sigma}_{\sigma,\sigma^{\prime}}\cdot\mathbf{\sigma}_{\rho^{\prime},\rho},\]

where \(\mathbf{\sigma}\) is the Pauli matrix vector. \(\hat{I}^{\varsigma(\varsigma)}\) is the spin (charge) channel interaction given as \(I^{\varsigma(\varsigma)}_{ll^{\prime}mm^{\prime}}=-g^{m^{\prime}l^{\prime}}_{ k^{\prime}-k}(k)g^{lm}_{ll^{\prime}-k^{\prime}}(k^{\prime}+q)(2V)^{2}\chi^{b, \varsigma(\varsigma)}(k-k^{\prime})\), where \(\chi^{b,\varsigma(\varsigma)}(q)\approx\frac{\chi^{00}_{\rm AB,AB}(q)}{1- \alpha^{\varsigma(\varsigma)}(q)}\), and \(\alpha^{\varsigma(\varsigma)}(q)\) is the spin (charge) channel Stoner factor. Based on Supplementary Eq. (17), the MT kernel function for the charge-channel form factor is given as \(\hat{I}^{\rm MT}=\frac{1}{2}(\hat{I}^{c}+3\hat{I}^{s})\)[45]. When \(v=0\), \(\alpha_{\rm s,c}(q)\) is equal to \(\alpha_{\rm BO}(q)\) and therefore \(\hat{I}^{c}=\hat{I}^{s}\). As a result, \(\hat{I}^{\rm MT}=2\hat{I}^{c}\) for \(v=0\), and therefore \(y=2\).

Here, we consider the effect of the BO interaction \(v\). When \(v>0\), the charge Stoner factor is magnified as \(\alpha_{\rm c}(q)=\alpha_{\rm BO}(q)+v\chi^{0}_{g}(\mathbf{q})\), where \(\chi^{0}_{g}(\mathbf{q})\) is the irreducible susceptibility given by Eq. (5) in the main text. Thus, \(\hat{I}^{c}\) is enlarged by \(v\). In contrast, the spin Stoner factor and \(\hat{I}^{s}\) are unchanged by \(v\). Even if \(v\) is small but finite (_e.g._, \(v\chi^{0}_{g}(\mathbf{q})\sim 0.1\)), the relation \(\hat{I}^{c}\gg\hat{I}^{s}\) will be realized near the charge-channel BO criticality. Therefore, we obtain \(\hat{I}^{\rm MT}\approx\frac{1}{2}\hat{I}^{c}\), which means that \(y\gtrsim 1/2\). To summarize, the relation \(y\gtrsim 1/2\) is generally expected when \(V\) and \(v\) (= AL interference mechanism and \(e\)-ph interaction) coexist.

Supplementary Note 3: Self-consistent DW equation method: derivation of renormalized BO fluctuations

In the main text, we discuss the development of the BO susceptibility \(\chi_{g}\) and its essential role in the cLC order. Based on the BO interaction model in Eq. (2) in the main text, we find that the MT term due to \(\chi_{g}\), \(I^{\rm MT}\sim-\chi_{g}\), leads to the emergence of the cLC order. On the other hand, \(I^{\rm MT}\) also induces the renormalization of the \(\chi_{g}\) itself. Here, we explain that the latter effect does not change the results of the main text. The kernel function composed of the Hartree and MT terms, shown in Supplementary Fig. 4**a**, are respectively given as

\[\tilde{I}^{ll^{\prime},mm^{\prime}}_{\rm MT,\mathbf{q}}(k,p) = -g^{m^{\prime}l^{\prime}}_{\mathbf{p}-\mathbf{k}}(\mathbf{k})g^{ lm}_{\mathbf{k}-\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{p}+\mathbf{q}) \tag{18}\] \[\times y\tilde{v}(1+\tilde{v}\tilde{\chi}_{g}(k-p)),\] \[I^{ll^{\prime},mm^{\prime}}_{\rm H,\mathbf{q}}(k,p) = g^{ll^{\prime}}_{\mathbf{q}}(k)vg^{mm^{\prime}}_{\mathbf{q}}(p) ^{*}, \tag{19}\]

where \(\tilde{\chi}_{g}(q)=\chi^{0}_{g}(q)/(1-\tilde{v}\chi^{0}_{g}(q))\) is the renormalized BO susceptibility. In the main text, \(v\) in the DW equation for the cLC form factor and in the self-energy is considered as the renormalized \(\tilde{v}\).

Here, by referring to the SCR theory [10], we perform the self-consistent calculation of the renormalized BO susceptibility, \(\tilde{\chi}_{g}(q)\), based on the DW equation. A natural self-consistency condition for the MT term composed of BO susceptibility is

\[\lambda_{\mathbf{q}_{1}}=\tilde{\alpha}_{\rm BO}, \tag{20}\]

where the left-hand side is the eigenvalue of the BO type form factor, and \(\tilde{\alpha}_{\rm BO}\equiv\tilde{v}\chi^{0}_{g}(\mathbf{q}_{1})\) is the renormalized BO Stoner factor. Then, we can calculate the DW equation under the self-consistent condition of BO fluctuations. \(\tilde{v}\) given by Supplementary Eq. (20) is expressed as

\[\tilde{v}=v+v^{\prime}, \tag{21}\] \[v^{\prime}=\frac{\frac{T^{2}}{N^{2}}\sum_{k,p,L,M}A^{L}_{g^{ \prime}}(k+\mathbf{q},-\mathbf{q})I^{L,M}_{\rm MT,\mathbf{q}}(k,p)A^{M}_{g^{ \prime}}(p,\mathbf{q})}{(\chi^{0}_{g^{\prime}}(\mathbf{q}))^{2}},\] (22) \[\hat{A}_{g^{\prime}}(p,\mathbf{q})=\hat{G}(p+\mathbf{q})\hat{g}^{ \prime}_{\mathbf{q}}(p)\hat{G}(p), \tag{23}\]

at \(\mathbf{q}=\mathbf{q}_{1}\), where \(g^{\prime}\) is the solution of the BO type form factor. The kernel function \(I_{\mathbf{q}}(k,p)\) is shown in Supplementary Fig. 4**a**. It is composed of the Hartree and MT terms. (Fock term is included in the MT term.) Because \(g^{\prime}\approx g\), \(\tilde{v}\) is simply derived from Supplementary Eq. (21) by setting \(g^{\prime}=g\). By using \(\tilde{v}\), we solve the DW equation and show the obtained \(\lambda_{\rm BO}(=\tilde{\alpha}_{\rm BO})\) and \(\lambda_{\rm cLC}\) for \(y=1\) and \(T=0.01\) in Supplementary Fig. 4**b**.

For small \(v\) (\(\lesssim 0.2\)), the DW equation solution in Supplementary Fig. 4**b** corresponds to the HF approximation. When \(f_{\mathbf{q}}(k)\) is BO form factor, the eigenvalue due to the Hartree term is equal to \(\alpha_{\rm BO}=1.34v\). However, it is reduced to \(\lambda_{\rm BO}=1.34(v+v^{\prime})\), where \(v^{\prime}=-0.31vv\) is the Fock term contribution. When \(f_{\mathbf{q}}(k)\) is the cLC form factor, the Hartree term vanishes, while the Fock term gives positive eigenvalue \(\lambda_{\rm cLC}=1.34v^{\prime\prime}\) with \(v^{\prime\prime}=0.26yv\). For large \(v\) (\(\gtrsim 1\)), the MT term becomes significant. For this reason, \(\lambda_{\rm BO}\) saturates while \(\lambda_{\rm cLC}\) strongly increases, and \(\lambda_{\rm cLC}\) reaches unity at \(v\approx 1.35\). This is because both \(-v^{\prime}\) and \(v^{\prime\prime}\) are strongly enlarged by the MT term when \(\tilde{\alpha}_{\rm BO}\lesssim 1\).

Supplementary Figures 4**c** and **d** show the obtained \(T_{\rm cLC}\) and \(T_{\rm BO}\) by solving the present DW equation, in the case of **c**\(y=1\) and **d**\(y=0.5\). Here, \(T_{\rm cLC}\) is defined by the condition \(\lambda_{{\bf q}_{1}}=1\) for the cLC type form factor. Also, \(T_{\rm BO}\) is defined as \(\lambda_{{\bf q}_{1}}=0.985\) for the BO type form factor. The overall \(v\)-dependences of the order parameters are similar to those in Figs. 4\({\bf d}\) and \({\bf e}\) in the main text. By introducing the self-energy, both \(T_{\rm cLC}\) and \(T_{\rm BO}\) will be suppressed, and the relation \(T_{\rm cLC}<T_{\rm BO}\) will be realized in the strong coupling region.

Supplementary Figure 4\({\bf e}\) presents the beyond-RPA processes \(\Delta\chi_{w}(q)\) in the present study. The total susceptibility is \(\chi_{w}^{\rm tot}(q)=\chi_{w}^{\rm RPA}(q)+\Delta\chi_{w}(q)\), All these diagrams are generated by solving the DW equation. For \(w=g\) (=BO form factor), \(\chi_{g}^{\rm RPA}(q)\) is large and positive, while \(\Delta\chi_{g}(q)\) takes negative values due to the MT terms. For \(w=f\) (=cLC form factor), \(\chi_{f}^{\rm RPA}(q)\) is very small, while \(\Delta\chi_{f}(q)\) takes positive values, which becomes significant when \(\alpha_{\rm BO}\sim 1\). Therefore, the cLC susceptibility \(\chi_{f}^{\rm tot}(q)\) develops as large as the BO susceptibility in the present theory.

In the main text, we solve the DW equation with including the self-energy, while we solve the DW equation under the self-consistent condition in Supplementary Eq. (20) in this section. We find that two different DW equation analyses with MT-type kernel function produce essentially equivalent numerical results. The present self-consistent DW equation analysis strongly supports the reliability of the numerical study in the main text.

## Supplementary Note 4: Effects of AL-type VCs

Here, we examine the role of the Aslamazov-Larkin (AL) vertex corrections (VCs) due to the interference between two bosonic susceptibilities (\(\chi^{\rm boson}\)) shown in Supplementary Fig. 5. The AL terms are significant for the even-parity order parameter in the paramagnon-interference mechanism. This mechanism is responsible for the BO and the orbital order in Fe-based superconductors [2; 3; 9], high-\(T_{c}\) cuprates [5; 6; 11], and kagome metals [1]. In contrast, the AL term is unimportant for the odd-parity order parameter, and instead, the MT term is significant for the current order in the frustrated Hubbard models [7] and non-Fermi liquid transport phenomena [8].

Here, we explain that the AL terms due to the bond susceptibilities, which were neglected in the main text, are unimportant in the present cLC mechanism in kagome metals. Supplementary Figure 5 exhibits the VC for \(f_{{\bf q}_{1}}^{\rm AB}({\bf k})\) at \({\bf k}\approx{\bf k}_{\rm A}\). These terms are almost canceled for the odd-parity cLC order because of the relation \(f_{{\bf q}_{1}}^{\rm AB}({\bf k})=-f_{{\bf q}_{1}}^{\rm BA}(-{\bf k}-{\bf q})\). In fact, the order parameter in the real space satisfies the relation \(\delta t_{ij}={\cal P}\delta t_{ji}\), where \({\cal P}=+1\) (\(-1\)) for the even (odd) parity order and \(l,m={\rm A,B,C}\). Then, its Fourier transform gives the form factor:

\[f_{{\bf q}}^{lm}({\bf k}) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}^{\rm sub-}\sum_{j}^{\rm sub-}\delta t_{ij}e^{ -{\bf k}\cdot({\bf r}_{i}-{\bf r}_{j})}e^{-{\bf q}\cdot{\bf r}_{j}} \tag{24}\] \[= \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}^{\rm sub-}i\sum_{j}^{\rm sub-}({\cal P}\delta t _{ji})e^{-{\bf k}\cdot({\bf r}_{i}-{\bf r}_{j})}e^{-{\bf q}\cdot{\bf r}_{j}}\] \[= {\cal P}f_{{\bf q}}^{ml}(-{\bf k}-{\bf q}).\]

In addition, it is verified that each AL term in Supplementary Fig. 5 is small because the momentum summation is restricted by four \(g\)'s. (Note that \(|g_{{\bf q}_{1}}^{lm}({\bf k})|\leq 1\).)

