## Supplementary Note 5: Parquet RG theory for kagome metals: Derivation of cLC and BO instabilities

In the main text, we revealed the BO fluctuation-mediated cLC mechanism in kagome metals based on the DW equation method. The BO fluctuations in the MT term of the DW equation causes the scattering between three van-Hove singularity (vHS) points. It is found that the cLC and BO fluctuations develop cooperatively, as demonstrated in Figs. 4**c**-**f** in the main text and Supplementary Fig. 4**b**-**d**.

The aim of this section is to verify the idea of the BO fluctuation-mediated cLC based on a different reliable theoretical framework. Here, we study the kagome lattice model based on the parquet renormalization group (RG) formulation [13; 14]. Supplementary Figure 6**a** represents the four scattering processes between three vHS points. \(g_{1}\) is the backward scattering, \(g_{3}\) is the Umklapp scattering, and \(g_{2}\) and \(g_{4}\) are the forward scatterings. A great merit of the RG method is that both particle-particle and particle-hole channels are treated on the same footing, while the details of the shape of the FS are dropped. Using \(g_{i}\)'s, \(\Gamma_{\rm um}\) and \(\Gamma_{\rm back}\) included in the kernel of the DW equation, which are introduced in the main text, are expressed in Supplementary Fig. 6**b**. The BO (cLC) instability is given by \(\Gamma_{\rm back}+(-)\Gamma_{\rm um}\).

Here, we solve the following parquet RG equation for the three vHS points model to obtain the renormalized \(g_{i}\) (\(i=1\sim 4\)) due to the electron correlation [13; 14]:

\[\frac{g_{1}}{dz} = 2dg_{1}(g_{2}-g_{1}), \tag{25}\] \[\frac{g_{2}}{dz} = 2d(g_{2}^{2}+g_{3}^{2}),\] (26) \[\frac{g_{3}}{dz} = -g_{3}^{2}-2g_{3}g_{4}+2dg_{3}(2g_{2}-g_{1}),\] (27) \[\frac{g_{4}}{dz} = -2g_{3}^{2}-g_{4}^{2}, \tag{28}\]

where \(z=\Pi^{0,{\rm AAAA}}(\mathbf{0},E)\sim\ln^{2}(E_{0}/E)\) and \(d=d\chi^{0,{\rm ABBA}}(\mathbf{q}_{1},E)/dz\). Here, \(\Pi^{0,{\rm AAAA}}(\mathbf{0},E)\) is the Cooper channel bubble with the low-energy (high-energy) cutoff \(E\) (\(E_{0}\)), and we consider the effect of the vHS at \(E_{\rm F}\). Note that \(E\) corresponds to \(\sim T\), so \(T\approx E_{0}\exp(-z^{1/2})\). When \(E_{0}=0.2\)eV, \(z=10\) corresponds to \(T\approx 100\)K. The parameter \(d\) is bounded \(0<d<1/2\), and \(d=1/2\) corresponds to the perfect nesting. Hereafter, we set \(d=1/4\) because the nesting of the real FS is not perfect. In this study, strong cLC and BO instabilities are robustly obtained for \(d=1/4\sim 1/2\).

The BO interaction in Eq. (2) in the main text gives the following initial values at \(z=0\) (_i.e._, \(E=E_{0}\)): \(g_{1}^{0}=g_{3}^{0}=-v/2\), \(g_{2}^{0}=0\). They correspond to \(y=1/2\) in the main text. Similar sets of \(\{g_{i}^{0}\}\) were discussed in Ref. [13]. In addition, we include the on-site Coulomb interaction \(g_{4}=U\). Supplementary Figure 6**c** shows the obtained flows of \(g_{i}\)'s as functions of \(z\) (\(\geq 0\)) for \(v=0.6\) and \(U=3\). In this case, the renormalized \(g_{1}\) takes a large negative value, while other \(g_{i}\)'s approach zero. In this case, the interaction for the \(X\)-channel susceptibility, \(G_{X}\), is shown in Supplementary Fig. 6**d**. \(G_{X}\) is given as [13; 14]\(G_{\rm eBO}=-2g_{1}+g_{2}-g_{3}\), \(G_{\rm cLC}=-2g_{1}+g_{2}+g_{3}\), \(G_{\rm sBO}=g_{2}+g_{3}\), \(G_{\rm sLC}=g_{2}-g_{3}\), \(G_{\rm s-SC}=-2g_{3}-g_{4}\), and \(G_{\rm d-SC}=g_{3}-g_{4}\). Here, \(X=\)(s)BO: charge (spin) bond order, \(X=\)c(s)LC: charge (spin) loop-current, \(X=s(d)\)-SC: \(s(d)\)-wave SC. Thus, both \(G_{\rm eBO}\) and \(G_{\rm eLC}\) strongly develop. The relation \(G_{\rm eBO}\approx G_{\rm eLC}\) is obtained because \(g_{3}\) is irrelevant. Therefore, we find \(T_{\rm cBC}\approx T_{\rm cLC}\).

Supplementary Figure 6**e** shows the obtained flow of \(g_{i}\) for \(v=0.6\) and \(U=0.7\). In this case, both \(|g_{3}|\) and \(|g_{1}|\) are enlarged. (\(|g_{1}|>|g_{3}|\) is satisfied.) The corresponding instability \(G_{X}\) is shown in Supplementary Fig. 6**f**. Thus, both \(G_{\rm eBO}\) and \(G_{\rm eLC}\) are strongly enlarged, while the relation \(G_{\rm eBO}\gtrsim G_{\rm eLC}\gtrsim G_{\rm s-SC}\) is obtained. Therefore,we find \(T_{\rm cBO}\gtrsim T_{\rm cLC}\gtrsim T_{s-{\rm SC}}\).

Note that the CDW instability for the onsite operator \(n_{\rm A}+sn_{\rm B}\) (\(s=\pm 1\)) at \({\bf q}={\bf q}_{1}\) is \(G_{\rm CDW,s}=s(g_{1}-2g_{2})-g_{4}\). Also, the SDW instability for the onsite operator \(m_{\rm A}+sm_{\rm B}\) (\(m_{\rm A}=n_{\rm A\uparrow}-n_{\rm A\downarrow}\)) at \({\bf q}={\bf q}_{1}\) is \(G_{\rm SDW,s}=sg_{1}+g_{4}\). Both quantities are smaller than \(G_{\rm cBO}\) and \(G_{\rm cLC}\) after the renormalization, as verified in Supplementary Figs. 6**d** and **f**.

To summarize, the strong cLC instability derived from the present RG study strongly indicates the validity of the BO fluctuation-mediated cLC mechanism in kagome metals. Considering the initial condition \(G_{\rm cLC}^{0}=G_{\rm cBO}^{0}/3=v/2\), the strong cLC instability originates from the beyond-RPA effect. The comparing study between the diagrammatic method and the RG method will lead to further interesting discoveries. On the other hand, parquet RG method cannot derive quantitative results, such as the long-range components of the order parameters obtained from the DW equations.

## Supplementary Note 6: Stability of the nematic BO+cLC state

### Supplementary Note 6-1: Analytic discussion

In the main text, we explained that the coexistence of the \(3Q\) BO and the \(3Q\) cLC leads to one \(C_{6}\) state and three nematic (\(C_{2}\)) states. Here, we discuss the stability of these four states based on the Ginzburg-Landau (GL) theory. It is found that the nematic states are expected to emerge when \(T_{\rm BO}>T_{\rm cLC}\) by considering the third-order GL terms, which play essential roles in kagome metals.

Here, we assume that the BO form factor \(g^{lm}\) is composed of the nearest-neighbor components, as we did in the main text. Then, the BO form factors \(g_{{\bf q}_{1}}^{\rm AB}({\bf k})\), \(g_{{\bf q}_{2}}^{\rm BC}\) and \(g_{{\bf q}_{3}}^{\rm CA}\) are given by Supplementary Eqs. (1)-(3), respectively. The relation \(g_{\bf q}^{lm}({\bf k})=\{g_{\bf q}^{ml}({\bf k})\}^{*}\) is satisfied. As for the cLC form factor \(f^{lm}\), we use the solution of the DW equation obtained in the main text. The obtained \(f^{lm}\) contains long-range components as shown in Fig. 3**c** in the main text. In fact, its \({\bf k}\)-space expression in Supplementary Figs. 8**a** and **b** is very different from Supplementary Eq. (1). In addition, the frequency dependence \(f^{lm}\) shown in Supplementary Fig. 8**c** is very strong, by reflecting drastic frequency dependence of the MT term (\(\propto-\chi_{g}(q)\)). The BO [cLC] parameter at \({\bf q}={\bf q}_{1}\) is \(\phi_{1}g_{\bf q}^{lm}\) [\(\eta_{1}f_{\bf q}^{lm}\)] with \(lm={\rm AB}\) or BA.

Here, we introduce the vector representation \(\mathbf{\phi}\equiv(\phi_{1},\phi_{2},\phi_{3})\), and define \(\mathbf{\phi}_{1}\equiv(\phi,\phi,\phi)/\sqrt{3}\) and \(\mathbf{\phi}_{2}\equiv(-\phi,\phi,\phi)/\sqrt{3}\). When \(\mathbf{\phi}=\mathbf{\phi}_{1}\), we obtain the Tri-Hexagonal (Star-of-David) pattern for \(\phi>0\) (\(\phi<0\)) shown in Supplementary Fig. 1**c**. (The free energy for \(\mathbf{\phi}\) is different from that for \(-\mathbf{\phi}\) due to the third-order term.) When \(\mathbf{\phi}=\mathbf{\phi}_{2}\), we obtain the Tri-Hexagonal pattern for \(\phi<0\), while it is displaced by \({\bf a}_{\rm AB}\) from Supplementary Fig. 1**c**.

We also introduce the notation \(\mathbf{\eta}\equiv(\eta_{1},\eta_{2},\eta_{3})\), and define \(\mathbf{\eta}_{1}\equiv(\eta,\eta,\eta)/\sqrt{3}\) and \(\mathbf{\eta}_{2}\equiv(-\eta,\eta,\eta)/\sqrt{3}\). The cLC pattern in Supplementary Fig. 1**d** is given by \(\mathbf{\eta}=\mathbf{\eta}_{1}\) with \(\eta>0\), and the direction of the cLC is reversed when \(\eta<0\). (The free energy is unchanged by \(\mathbf{\eta}\rightarrow-\mathbf{\eta}\).) When \(\mathbf{\eta}=\mathbf{\eta}_{2}\), the cLC pattern is given by the parallel shift of Supplementary Fig. 1**d** by \({\bf a}_{\rm AB}\).

Hereafter, we construct the GL free energy up to the fourth-order terms:

\[F = F^{(2)}+F^{(3)}+F^{(4)}. \tag{29}\]

The second-order term is

\[F^{(2)} = a_{1}|\mathbf{\phi}|^{2}+a_{2}|\mathbf{\eta}|^{2}, \tag{30}\] \[a_{1} = -\chi_{g}^{0}({\bf q}_{1})+I_{\rm b}^{-1},\] (31) \[a_{2} = -\chi_{f}^{0}({\bf q}_{1})+I_{\rm c}^{-1}, \tag{32}\]

where \(I_{\rm b(c)}\) (\(>0\)) is the effective interaction for the BO (cLC order) [11; 13]. (\(a_{1,2}\leq 0\) corresponds to \(\lambda_{\bf q}\geq 1\) in the DW equation as proved in Ref. [11].) Because \(\chi_{g}^{0}({\bf q}_{1})\approx\chi_{f}^{0}({\bf q}_{1})\), the relation \(T_{\rm BO}>T_{\rm cLC}\) would be realized when \(I_{\rm b}>I_{\rm c}\). We note that, in the present theory, both \(I_{\rm b}\) and \(I_{\rm c}\) originate from the electron correlations, and therefore they exhibit strong \(T\)-dependences.

The third-order term and the forth-order term for general \(\mathbf{\phi}\) and \(\mathbf{\eta}\) are given as

\[F^{(3)} = b_{1}\phi_{1}\phi_{2}\phi_{3}+b_{2}(\phi_{1}\eta_{2}\eta_{3}+ \eta_{1}\phi_{2}\eta_{3}+\eta_{1}\eta_{2}\phi_{3}), \tag{33}\] \[F^{(4)} = d_{1,a}(\phi_{1}^{4}+\phi_{2}^{4}+\phi_{3}^{4})+d_{1,b}(\phi_{1}^ {2}\phi_{2}^{2}+\phi_{2}^{2}\phi_{3}^{2}+\phi_{3}^{2}\phi_{1}^{2})\] \[+d_{2,a}(\eta_{1}^{4}+\eta_{2}^{4}+\eta_{3}^{4})+d_{2,b}(\eta_{1} ^{2}\eta_{2}^{2}+\eta_{2}^{2}\eta_{3}^{2}+\eta_{3}^{2}\eta_{1}^{2})\] \[+2d_{3,a}(\phi_{1}^{2}\eta_{1}^{2}+\phi_{2}^{2}\eta_{2}^{2}+\phi_{3 }^{2}\eta_{3}^{2})\] \[+d_{3,b}(\phi_{1}^{2}\eta_{2}^{2}+\phi_{2}^{2}\eta_{3}^{3}+\phi_{3 }^{2}\eta_{1}^{2}+\phi_{2}^{2}\eta_{1}^{2}+\phi_{3}^{2}\eta_{2}^{2}+\phi_{1}^{2} \eta_{3}^{2}).\]

Note that the term \(\sim\phi_{1}\phi_{2}\eta_{1}\eta_{2}\) is absent. In the absence of the cLC order, the third-order free energy term is given by the first term of Supplementary Eq. (33). The diagrammatic expression for the coefficient \(b_{1}\) is given in Supplementary Fig. 7**a**, and its analytic expression is found in Refs. [12; 13]. When the BO and the cLC coexist, we obtain the additional cross-terms given by the second term of Supplementary Eq. (33). The diagrammatic expression for \(b_{2}\) is given in Supplementary Fig. 7**b**. The relation \(b_{1}=-b_{2}\) holds when the relation \(f_{{\bf q}_{2}}^{\rm BC}({\bf k}_{\rm C})f_{{\bf q}_{3}}^{\rm CA}({\bf k}_{\rm A})=-g_{{ \bf q}_{2}}^{\rm BC}({\bf k}_{\rm C})g_{{\bf q}_{3}}^{\rm CA}({\bf k}_{\rm A})\) holds. Note that the minimum of the GL free energy does not diverge due to the positive fourth-order term.

Now, we set \(\mathbf{\phi}=\mathbf{\phi}_{1}\) because the \(3Q\) BO is stabilized by the third-order GL free energy (\(\propto\phi_{1}\phi_{2}\phi_{3}\)), and it is actually observed experimentally. When \(\mathbf{\eta}=\mathbf{\eta}_{1[2]}\), the BO+cLC state is \(C_{6}\) [\(C_{2}\)] symmetric as shown in Fig. 5**a** [**b**] in the main text. Hereafter, we explain that the \(C_{2}\)-symmetric BO+CLC state is realized due to \(\mathbf{\eta}\neq\mathbf{\eta}_{1}\), based on both analytic and numerical studies.

Here, we discuss the possible \(3Q\) BO+cLC states in the case of \(T_{\rm BO}>T_{\rm cLC}\). When \(\mathbf{\phi}=\mathbf{\phi}_{1}\), the third-order term is

\[F^{(3)^{\prime}} = \frac{b_{1}}{3\sqrt{3}}\phi^{3} \tag{35}\] \[+\frac{b_{2}}{2\sqrt{3}}\phi[(\eta_{1}+\eta_{2}+\eta_{3})^{2}-| \mathbf{\eta}|^{2}].\]

Now, we minimize Supplementary Eq. (35) under the constraints \(|\mathbf{\eta}|=\)const. and \(|\mathbf{\phi}|=\)const., where the 2nd order GL free energy is constant. In the case of \(|\mathbf{\eta}|\gg|\mathbf{\phi}|\) (\(T_{\rm cLC}\gg T_{\rm BO}\)), \(b_{2}\phi\) becomes negative and \(\mathbf{\eta}=\mathbf{\eta}_{1}\) to get the energy gain from the second term in Supplementary Eq. (35). (The first term is small and positive because of \(b_{1}b_{2}<0\).) Thus, the coexisting state has \(C_{6}\) symmetry.

In the case of \(|\mathbf{\eta}|\ll|\mathbf{\phi}|\) (\(T_{\rm cLC}\ll T_{\rm BO}\)), \(b_{1}\phi\) becomes negative to get the energy gain from the first term in Supplementary Eq. (35). Then, the second term is minimized when \(\eta_{1}+\eta_{2}+\eta_{3}=0\) for a fixed \(|\mathbf{\eta}|\). For example, \(\mathbf{\eta}^{\prime}\propto(\eta,\eta,-2\eta)\) or \(\mathbf{\eta}^{\prime\prime}\propto(\eta,-\eta,0)\). In both cases, the coexisting state has \(C_{2}\) symmetry. The nematic BO+cLC state with \(\mathbf{\phi}_{1}\) and \(\mathbf{\eta}^{\prime}\) is depicted in Fig. 5 **c** in the main text. Note that the total free energy is unchanged under \(\eta_{1}+\eta_{2}+\eta_{3}=0\) within the 4th-order GL terms, while this degeneracy is lifted by the 6th-order GL terms. Thus, the \(Z_{3}\) nematic BO+cLC state by \(\mathbf{\eta}^{\prime}\) or \(\mathbf{\eta}^{\prime\prime}\) is expected to be realized for \(T<T_{\rm cLC}<T_{\rm BO}\), as shown in a schematic phase diagram in Fig. 4 **f** in the main text.

Finally, we discuss the fourth-order GL term in Supplementary Eq. (34). The coefficients \(d_{l,a}\) and \(d_{l,b}\) (\(l=1,2,3\)) are positive, and they are given by the closed Feynman diagrams made of four form factors and four \(G\)'s [13]. The relations \(d_{1,a}=d_{2,a}=d_{3,a}\) and \(d_{1,b}=d_{2,b}=d_{3,b}\) hold in the case of \(f^{lm}_{\bf q}({\bf k})\approx\pm ig^{lm}_{\bf q}({\bf k})\). For any \(\mathbf{\phi}=\mathbf{\phi}_{\alpha}\) and \(\mathbf{\eta}=\mathbf{\eta}_{\beta}\) (\(\alpha,\beta=1,2\)), the fourth-order GL term is expressed as

\[F^{(4)^{\prime}}=d_{1}\phi^{4}+d_{2}\eta^{4}+2d_{3}\phi^{2}\eta^{2}, \tag{36}\]

where the coefficients are \(d_{1}=(d_{1,a}+d_{1,b})/3\), \(d_{2}=(d_{2,a}+d_{2,b})/3\), and \(d_{3}=(d_{3,a}+d_{3,b})/3\). However, the relation \(f^{lm}_{\bf q}({\bf k})\approx\pm ig^{lm}_{\bf q}({\bf k})\) is not satisfied because the mechanisms of the cLC and the BO are different in the present theory. Therefore, all the coefficients \(b_{l}\), \(d_{m,a}\), \(d_{m,b}\) depend on \(l=1,2\) and \(m=1,2,3\). In the successive section, we discuss the stability of the nematic BO+cLC state based on the numerical study.

#### Supplementary Note 6-2: Numerical study

Here, we calculate the 3rd- and 4th-order GL coefficients based on the diagrammatic method. We normalize the form factors as \(\max_{\bf k}|g^{\rm AB}_{{\bf q}_{1}}({\bf k})|=\max_{{\bf k},l,m}|f^{\rm AB} _{{\bf q}_{1}}({\bf k})|=1\) which is realized at \({\bf k}={\bf k}_{\rm A}\) in the present DW equation analysis. Then, the BO parameter \(\phi_{m}g_{{\bf q}_{m}}({\bf k})\) at \({\bf q}={\bf q}_{m}\) (\(m=1\sim 3\)) gives the hybridization gap \(\Delta_{\rm BO}\sim|\phi|\) in the folded band at \(\Gamma\) point. [In the same way, \(\Delta_{\rm cLC}\sim|\eta|\) for the cLC order \(\eta_{m}f_{{\bf q}_{m}}({\bf k})\).] Thus, the present normalization rule for \(g\) and \(f\) is physically reasonable and convenient. Note that the normalization rule does not influence the physical quantities, because the change of \(H_{\rm int}\) due to \(g\to r\cdot g\) is absorbed by \(v\to v/r^{2}\).

The 3rd order GL parameters per unit cell are given as

\[b_{1} = 3I^{gggg}_{123}+3I^{ggg}_{132}, \tag{37}\] \[b_{2} = 3I^{fff}_{123}+3I^{fff}_{132}, \tag{38}\]

where

\[I^{xyz}_{lmn} = -\frac{T}{3N}\sum_{k,\sigma}{\rm Tr}\hat{x}_{q_{l}}(k+q_{n}+q_{m} )\hat{G}(k+q_{n}+q_{m}) \tag{39}\] \[\times \hat{y}_{q_{m}}(k+q_{n})\hat{G}(k+q_{n})\hat{z}_{q_{n}}(k)\hat{G}(k),\]

where \(x,y,z\) is \(f\) or \(g\), and \(l,m,n\) is 1, 2, or 3. The relation \(q_{l}+q_{m}+q_{n}=0\) should be satisfied. Here, \(\hat{G}(k)\)is the \(3\times 3\) matrix expression of the Green function with the self-energy given in the main text.

The 4th order GL parameters per unit cell are given as

\[d_{1,a} = I_{1111}^{gggg}, \tag{40}\] \[d_{1,b} = 2I_{1212}^{gggg}+4I_{1122}^{gggg},\] (41) \[d_{2,a} = I_{1111}^{ffff},\] (42) \[d_{2,b} = 2I_{1212}^{ffff}+4I_{1122}^{ffff},\] (43) \[d_{3,a} = \frac{1}{2}(2I_{1111}^{fgfg}+4I_{1111}^{ffgg}),\] (44) \[d_{3,b} = 2I_{1212}^{fgfg}+4I_{1122}^{ffgg}, \tag{45}\]

where

\[I_{hlmn}^{wxyz} = \frac{T}{4N}\sum_{k,\sigma}\mathrm{Tr}\hat{w}_{q_{k}}(k+q_{n}+q_{ m}+q_{l})\hat{G}(k+q_{n}+q_{m}+q_{l}) \tag{46}\] \[\times \hat{x}_{q_{l}}(k+q_{n}+q_{m})\hat{G}(k+q_{n}+q_{m})\] \[\times \hat{y}_{q_{m}}(k+q_{n})\hat{G}(k+q_{n})\hat{z}_{q_{n}}(k)\hat{G }(k),\]

where \(w,x,y,z\) is \(f\) or \(g\), and \(h,l,m,n\) is 1, 2, or 3. The relation \(q_{h}+q_{l}+q_{m}+q_{n}=0\) should be satisfied. The diagrammatic expression of \(I_{lmn}^{xyz}\) and \(I_{hlmn}^{wxyz}\) are depicted in Supplementary Fig. 9.

Note that these GL coefficients depend on the Green functions (=bandstructure) and the form factors \(g\) and \(f\). Thus, the GL coefficients depend on the BO/cLC mechanisms indirectly, just through the form factors.

The numerical results of the 3rd and 4th GL parameters are shown in Supplementary Figs. 10 **a** and **b**, respectively, for \(v=0.6\) and \(y=1\). We find that \(b_{1}\) and \(b_{2}\) have different signs, consistently with the discussion in the previous section. The obtained GL coefficients exhibit moderate and monotonic \(T\)-dependences for a wide \(T\) range because the self-energy suppresses unrealistic singular behaviors of GL coefficients at low temperatures. As we show in Supplementary Fig. 10 **c**, the obtained ratio \(r=2d_{1,a}/d_{1,b}\) is larger than 1. This result means that experimentally observed \(3Q\) BO state is realized irrespective of the size of \(b_{1}\). Also, the relation \(r^{\prime}=2d_{2,a}/d_{2,b}>1\) means that the \(3Q\) cLC state is realized.

In addition, the obtained GL coefficients satisfy the relations \(R=d_{1,a}d_{2,a}/d_{3,a}^{2}>1\) and \(R^{\prime}=d_{1,b}d_{2,b}/d_{3,b}^{2}>1\) as shown in Supplementary Fig. 10 **d**. These relations indicate the smallness of the competition between cLC and BO described by \(d_{3,a}\) and \(d_{3,b}\) terms. (Note that \(R=R^{\prime}=1\) when the BO and cLC form factors are composed of only the nearest bonds [13].) Therefore, we can expect the coexistence of the BO and cLC orders by analyzing the GL free energy. The relations \(R,R^{\prime}>1\) originates from the smallness of the inner-product \(|\frac{1}{N}\sum_{\mathbf{k}}[ij_{\mathbf{q}}^{ml}(\mathbf{k})]^{*}g_{ \mathbf{q}}^{lm}(\mathbf{k})|\) (see Supplementary Fig. 8 and **b**) and the strong frequency dependence of the cLC form factor shown in Supplementary Fig. 8 **c**. This is not surprising because the driving forces of the BO and the cLC are different in the present theory.

Supplementary Figure 11 **a** exhibits the obtained phase diagram derived from the GL free energy, by using the GL coefficients in Supplementary Figs. 10 **a** and **b** at \(T=0.01\). The horizontal (vertical) axis is the second-order GL parameter for the BO \(a_{1}\) (cLC \(a_{2}\)). Each phase is determined by minimizing the GL free energy \(F[\mathbf{\phi},\mathbf{\eta}]\) with respect to the following \(13^{2}\) patterns \((\mathbf{\phi},\mathbf{\eta})=(\mathbf{\phi}_{m},\mathbf{\eta}_{n})\) with \(m,n=1\sim 13\) exactly numerically:

\[3Q\ \mathrm{BO}: \mathbf{\phi}_{1}=\phi(1,1,1)/\sqrt{3},\] \[\mathbf{\phi}_{2}=\phi(-1,1,1)/\sqrt{3},\ \ \mathbf{\phi}_{3},\ \mathbf{\phi}_{4}= \mathrm{cycl.},\] \[2Q\ \mathrm{BO}: \mathbf{\phi}_{5}=\phi(1,1,0)/\sqrt{2},\ \ \mathbf{\phi}_{6},\ \mathbf{\phi}_{7}= \mathrm{cycl.},\] \[\mathbf{\phi}_{8}=\phi(1,-1,0)/\sqrt{2},\ \ \mathbf{\phi}_{9},\ \mathbf{\phi}_{10}= \mathrm{cycl.},\]

Figure 9: Diagrammatic expressions of Ginzburg-Landau parameters: **a**. Third-order term. **b**. Fourth-order term.

\[1Q\ \mathrm{BO}:\ \ \ \mathbf{\phi}_{11}=\phi(1,0,0),\ \ \mathbf{\phi}_{12},\ \mathbf{\phi}_{13}= \mathrm{cycl.},\]

and \(\mathbf{\eta}_{n}=\mathbf{\phi}_{n}|_{\phi\to\eta}\) for the cLC.

The obtained \(C_{2}\) symmetric BO+cLC phase with \(\mathbf{\phi}=\mathbf{\phi}_{1}\) and \(\mathbf{\eta}=(\eta,-\eta,0)/\sqrt{2}\) (or equivalently \(\mathbf{\eta}=(\eta,\eta,-2\eta)/\sqrt{6}\); see E-1) is shown by the purple region. This \(C_{2}\) coexisting phase is always realized when \(T_{\mathrm{BO}}\gtrsim T_{\mathrm{cLC}}\). Also, the green (red) region corresponds to \(3Q\) BO (cLC) phase. In the red region, the secondary \(3Q\) BO appears through the \(b_{2}\)-terms even for \(a_{1}>0\), and \(|\mathbf{\phi}|\) becomes comparable to \(|\mathbf{\eta}|\) when \(a_{1}<0\). The coexisting state is \(C_{6}\) symmetry. [13].

In deriving the phase boundary between the \(C_{2}\) and \(C_{6}\) coexisting phases in Supplementary Fig. 11 **a**, we optimized three parameters \(\{\phi,\phi^{\prime},\eta\}\) numerically by considering additional 14th order parameter \(\mathbf{\phi}_{14}\sim(\phi,\phi,\phi^{\prime})\) and \(\mathbf{\eta}\sim(\eta,-\eta,0)\). The dotted line in Supplementary Fig. 11 **a** is the phase boundary by setting \(\phi=\phi^{\prime}\).

Supplementary Figure 11 **b** shows the obtained order parameters along the red broken line in **a**. Here, we set 2nd order GL parameters as functions of \(T\): \(a_{1}=A_{1}(T/T_{\mathrm{BO}}^{0}-1)\), \(a_{2}=A_{2}(T/T_{\mathrm{cLC}}^{0}-1)\), where \(A_{1}=A_{2}=0.17\), \(T_{\mathrm{BO}}^{0}=0.01\) and \(T_{\mathrm{cLC}}^{0}=0.85T_{\mathrm{BO}}^{0}\). Here, \(A_{1(2)}=0\) at \(T=T_{\mathrm{BO}(\mathrm{cLC})}^{0}\). The GL parameters at \(T=0.01\) in Supplementary Fig. 10 are used. We find that \(|\mathbf{\phi}|\) exhibits the 1st order transition at \(T_{\mathrm{BO}}\approx 1.1T_{\mathrm{BO}}^{0}\), and \(|\mathbf{\eta}|\) appears as the 2nd-order transition at \(T_{\mathrm{cLC}}\approx 0.6T_{\mathrm{cLC}}^{0}\approx 0.5T_{\mathrm{BO}}^{0}\). This result corresponds to the region \(v>v^{*}\) in Fig. 4 f in the main text. By performing careful numerical analyses, we find that the \(C_{2}\) coexisting region (=purple region in Supplementary Figure 11 **a**) appears when the relations \(R>1\) and \(R^{\prime}>1\) are satisfied.

Supplementary Figure 11 **c** shows the order parameters along the green broken line in **a**. The primary \(3Q\) cLC order induces the secondary \(3Q\) BO due to the 3rd order GL terms. The symmetry of this coexisting phase is \(C_{6}\)[13].

